From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AN7581 WED downlink work <noreply@localhost>
Date: Thu, 02 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] airoha: dts: an7581: describe the MT7622-derived WED/WDMA blocks

Airoha AN7581 carries two instances of the MediaTek MT7622 Wi-Fi
Offload Engine plus a companion WDMA block. They are the SoC-side peer
of the "mt_whnat" WOE/WED driver and enable the Wi-Fi TX (downlink)
hardware offload path that mainline mt76 already knows how to drive
through mtk_wed_device_attach() for mt7915.

Both nodes are added disabled; boards opt in explicitly. Only the
FiberHome HG5585F CT enables them today, it carries the MT7916D whose
two PCIe HIFs line up with the two WED instances.

Nothing in the current tree claims these compatibles, so adding the
nodes alone changes no behaviour. The nodes become live only once a
driver binds, which is what the companion package airoha-wed is for.

The nodes also carry airoha,wed-ops / airoha,attach-enable here, i.e.
they are armed as soon as a board sets status = "okay" -- which is what
the stock H3C device does (mt_whnat is loaded and its IRQ is firing).
Boards opt in explicitly (see the companion enable patch); this one only
describes the hardware.

---
diff --git a/target/linux/airoha/dts/an7581-wed.dtsi b/target/linux/airoha/dts/an7581-wed.dtsi
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/target/linux/airoha/dts/an7581-wed.dtsi
@@ -0,0 +1,123 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+
+#include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/irq.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/arm-gic.h>
+
+/*
+ * Airoha AN7581 (= EN7581) integrates two instances of the MediaTek MT7622
+ * WED (Wi-Fi Offload Engine) IP, plus a companion WDMA block.
+ *
+ * This is the SoC-side peer of the "mt_whnat" WOE/WED driver. Together with
+ * mainline mt76's existing mt7915 WED hooks it enables the
+ * Wi-Fi TX (downlink) hardware offload path:
+ *
+ *   PPE/FOE --force_port=FP_WDMA(3)--> WDMA --> WED --> PCIe --> MT7916
+ *
+ * Default status is "disabled"; each board opts in explicitly. Enabling on a
+ * board whose MT7916 firmware does not cooperate would only waste power --
+ * nothing else in the SoC binds to these compatibles.
+ *
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * Provenance
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * Addresses / IRQs: merbanan/airoha_ml "en7581-base.dtsi".
+ *   Cross-checked: its pcie0 is 0x1fc00000 + GIC_SPI 39, byte-for-byte
+ *   identical to OpenWrt an7581.dtsi pcie0. Independent sources agree, and
+ *   the IRQ range 67-74 lands in a hole unused by an7581.dtsi
+ *   (which consumes 18-64 and 103-170).
+ *
+ * Register layout: matches MediaTek's wed_def.h from the airoha_wed_wdma
+ *   (mt_whnat) tree exactly:
+ *     WED_REV 0x000 | WED_MOD_RST 0x008 | WED_CTRL 0x00c
+ *     WED_AXI_CTRL 0x010 | WED_CTRL2 0x01c | WED_EX_INT_STA 0x020
+ *
+ * Hardware confirmed on-device 2026-10-02 via devmem:
+ *   0x1fa02000 -> 0x76220001   WED_REV = MT7622 rev 1
+ *   0x1fa03000 -> 0x76220001   second instance, same IP
+ *   0x1fa0200c -> 0x01000000   WED_CTRL
+ *   0x1fa0201c -> 0x00000020   WED_CTRL2
+ *   0x1fa06200 -> 0x3c000204   WDMA responds too
+ * Reads are clean (no bus abort), so the block is mapped and clocked.
+ *
+ * WDMA instance size 0x400 is not arbitrary: mt_whnat's wdma.h encodes each
+ * instance as two 0x200 halves (WDMA0_OFST0/1 = 0x6204_6220 / 0x6100_6000
+ * relative to CFG_BASE 0x1fa00000), i.e. 0x1fa06000 + 0x1fa06200. One 0x400
+ * window covers exactly that pair.
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ */
+
+/ {
+	soc {
+		wed: wed@1fa02000 {
+			compatible = "en751221,wed";
+			reg = <0x0 0x1fa02000 0x0 0xb00>,
+			      <0x0 0x1fa03000 0x0 0xb00>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 67 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 68 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			/* number of WED instances, read via of_property_read_u32_index */
+			wed_num = <2>;
+			/* WED instance -> PCIe slot carrying that MT7916 HIF */
+			pci_slot_map = <0 1>;
+
+			/*
+			 * Publish the mtk_soc_wed_ops table. Off by default:
+			 * without it mt76's forward path sees
+			 * mtk_wed_device_active() == false and Wi-Fi stays
+			 * software-only.
+			 *
+			 * This has to be a device-tree property rather than a
+			 * module parameter. The node is "okay", so the kernel
+			 * autoloads airoha-wed by modalias at t=3.38s on this
+			 * board -- before preinit (3.75s) and before procd reads
+			 * /etc/modules.d (10.4s). Module arguments supplied there
+			 * are silently dropped because the module is already
+			 * resident. Verified on-device: modules.d with explicit
+			 * arguments still gave wed_ops=N, while
+			 * "insmod .../airoha-wed.ko wed_ops=1" gave wed_ops=Y.
+			 *
+			 * airoha,attach-enable additionally lets attach() succeed.
+			 * Leaving it off programs the hardware exactly as a real
+			 * attach would, then rolls back and refuses, so the Wi-Fi
+			 * datapath is unchanged. It is what makes the bring-up
+			 * reversible without reflashing.
+			 *
+			 * Now set. On-device 2026-10-03, via
+			 * "echo 1 > .../airoha_wed/parameters/attach_enable"
+			 * plus an insmod with wed_enable=1:
+			 *   WED0 attached
+			 *   WED0 started (irq_mask=0xee0e000b)
+			 *   WED_CTRL 0x01000000 -> 0x01000507 (TX_BM + TX_FREE_AGT)
+			 * with both APs coming up and an associated client at
+			 * 780 Mbit/s VHT-MCS9. The PCIe map landed on bus 1 /
+			 * 0x1fc20000 as intended. One-shot WED_EX_INT_STA
+			 * 0x00380000 is reported and clears itself; it did not
+			 * stop the datapath.
+			 *
+			 * Note: this alone is not enough to offload. mt7915e has
+			 * a second gate, its own wed_enable module parameter,
+			 * which /etc/modules.d/mt7915e already sets to 1. Both
+			 * must be true. Note also that modprobe drops that
+			 * argument on this platform; only an explicit
+			 * "insmod .../mt7915e.ko wed_enable=1" works.
+			 */
+			airoha,wed-ops;
+			airoha,attach-enable;
+
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+
+		wdma: wdma@1fa06000 {
+			compatible = "en751221,wdma";
+			reg = <0x0 0x1fa06000 0x0 0x400>,
+			      <0x0 0x1fa06400 0x0 0x400>;
+			/* 3 IRQs per instance (WDMA_IRQ_NUM == 3 in wdma.h) */
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 69 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 70 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 71 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 72 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 73 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 74 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			status = "disabled";
+		};
+	};
+};
diff --git a/target/linux/airoha/dts/an7581.dtsi b/target/linux/airoha/dts/an7581.dtsi
--- a/target/linux/airoha/dts/an7581.dtsi
+++ b/target/linux/airoha/dts/an7581.dtsi
@@ -8,5 +8,6 @@
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/airoha,en7581-reset.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/thermal/thermal.h>
+#include "an7581-wed.dtsi"
 
 / {
-- 
2.45.2
